{
  "result": "I want to talk about libraries at closing time, when the lights hum a little lower and the shelves seem to exhale. There is a particular smell of paper and dust and coffee gone cold that feels like a promise. I love wandering those aisles with no real destination, letting my fingers trail across spines until one title tugs back. It never fails to surprise me how a book I did not know I needed finds me exactly when my brain is noisy. Last week it was a battered field guide to clouds, full of margin notes from a stranger who really loved cirrus. I sat cross-legged on the floor and read about mare\u2019s tails and mackerel skies while the librarian shelved returns around me. For twenty minutes my phone stayed in my pocket, my to-do list dissolved, and I was just a person learning sky vocabulary from another person\u2019s pencil. That is the magic I keep coming back for, this quiet collaboration across time between strangers who will never meet. Libraries remind me that curiosity is not efficient and does not need to be, that wandering is a perfectly valid research method. In a world obsessed with optimization and answers, I cherish a place designed for lingering questions and happy accidents. So if you need me, I will be in the 500s, sitting on the floor, looking up even while indoors. Come join me sometime; we can learn the names of clouds together and then go find them outside.",
